 pickg=[
  ("Humans for centuries {have dreamed|dreamed} of machines that could become intelligent and make human-like decisions.",
   "정답 ▶ have dreamed\n이유: for centuries(수 세기 동안 지금까지) → 과거부터 현재까지 이어지는 현재완료"),
  ("There have been myths about robots, automatons, and artificial beings since ancient Greece (e.g., the myth of Pandora, {who|which} released ills upon the world).",
   "정답 ▶ who\n이유: 선행사 Pandora(사람) → 주격 관계대명사 who (콤마 뒤 계속적 용법)"),
  ("In 1950, British mathematician Alan Turing asked {whether|that} machines could think and reason like humans and then developed the Turing test to measure a machine's intelligence and whether the machines can think autonomously.",
   "정답 ▶ whether\n이유: ask(묻다)의 목적어로 '~인지 (아닌지)' → whether/if\n오답 ▶ that은 '~라는 것'이라 질문 내용이 될 수 없어요."),
  ("A few years later, MIT professor John McCarthy coined \"artificial intelligence,\" {replacing|replaced} the previously used expression \"automata studies.\"",
   "정답 ▶ replacing\n이유: McCarthy(의 새 용어)가 이전 표현을 '대체하는' 능동 → 현재분사(분사구문)"),
  ("Since then, artificial intelligence {has become|became} the study and practice of \"making intelligent machines\" that are programmed to think like humans — endowed by their creators with reasoning and learning.",
   "정답 ▶ has become\n이유: Since then(그 이후로 지금까지) → 현재완료"),
  ("The slow-moving creature hibernates during the winter and stays in its tunnel for much of the summer, {meaning|meant} that it spends more than 90 percent of its life immobile.",
   "정답 ▶ meaning\n이유: 앞 내용 전체가 '~라는 뜻이다'(능동) → 분사구문 meaning (= which means)"),
  ("The tortoise stocks up on water by {eating|eat} plants and digging holes to collect rain.",
   "정답 ▶ eating\n이유: 전치사 by 뒤 → 동명사. eating과 digging이 병렬이에요."),
  ("But to stay {supplied|supplying} with water through its extended hibernation, the reptile relies on something else — its highly sophisticated bladder.",
   "정답 ▶ supplied\n이유: stay + p.p. = ~된 상태로 있다. 거북이 물을 '공급받는' 수동 → supplied"),
  ("Unlike most animals, the tortoise's bladder acts as a holding tank, allowing it {to reabsorb|reabsorbing} water back into its body.",
   "정답 ▶ to reabsorb\n이유: allow + 목적어 + to부정사 = ~가 …하게 해 주다"),
  ("And because its bladder is so important to a tortoise's survival, park rangers often remind visitors {not to stop|not stopping} and help the slow-movers across the road.",
   "정답 ▶ not to stop\n이유: remind + 목적어 + to부정사. 부정은 to 앞에 not → not to stop"),
  ("Tortoises become {so|such} terrified when people pick them up that they empty their bladders, losing their precious water reserves.",
   "정답 ▶ so\n이유: so + 형용사 + that ~ = 너무 …해서 ~하다\n비교 ▶ such + (a) + 형용사 + 명사 + that"),
  ("Because the brakes change your movement, {making|made} you slow down more suddenly, they must be exerting a force on the bicycle and you, as they grip and rub on the wheel-rims.",
   "정답 ▶ making\n이유: 브레이크가 여러분을 느려지게 '만드는' 능동 → 분사구문 making"),
  ("This is the force {called|calling} friction, which tends to slow down moving things by acting in the direction opposite to movement, that is backwards.",
   "정답 ▶ called\n이유: 힘이 마찰력이라고 '불리는' 수동 → the force (which is) called friction"),
  ("Even without the brakes on, there {are|is} other friction forces acting on you and your bicycle, which also slow you down.",
   "정답 ▶ are\n이유: there + be동사 뒤의 명사(other friction forces, 복수)가 진짜 주어 → are"),
 ],
